  "turns": [
    {
      "_id": "0",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "Let's read the problem: solve 5 times the quantity x minus 3, equal to 2x plus 9, for x.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t001"
    },
    {
      "_id": "1",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 2,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "So we have 5(x - 3) = 2x + 9, and we need to find x.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t002"
    },
    {
      "_id": "2",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 3,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "Good, that's exactly what's given — now let's see how to work with it.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t003"
    },
    {
      "_id": "3",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 4,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "So x shows up on both sides, that's going to take some untangling.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t004"
    },
    {
      "_id": "4",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 5,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "Right, the first thing to deal with is that bracket on the left — remember the distributive property?",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t005"
    },
    {
      "_id": "5",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 6,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "Yeah, multiplying 5 by both x and negative 3 inside the parentheses.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t006"
    },
    {
      "_id": "6",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 7,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "Exactly, that turns the left side into a simple expression with one x term.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t007"
    },
    {
      "_id": "7",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 8,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "And the right side already has its own x term, 2x.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t008"
    },
    {
      "_id": "8",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 9,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "So once we expand, we'll have x terms on both sides that need combining.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t009"
    },
    {
      "_id": "9",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 10,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "Makes sense — the goal is to get all the x's on one side and the numbers on the other.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t010"
    },
    {
      "_id": "10",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 11,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "Before we go further, let's make sure we've got the original equation exactly right.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t011"
    },
    {
      "_id": "11",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 12,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "It's 5 times the quantity x minus 3, equal to 2x plus 9.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t012"
    },
    {
      "_id": "12",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 13,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "Perfect, that's the equation we're working with — let's think about our approach.",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t013"
    },
    {
      "_id": "13",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 14,
      "role": "STUDENT_1",
      "content": "Maybe it doesn't matter whether we expand first or move terms first?",
      "turn_id": "demo-b-s03-t014"
    },
    {
      "_id": "14",
      "session_id": "demo-b-s03",
      "sequence_id": 15,
      "role": "TUTOR",
      "content": "That's a good question to test out — let's see if both orders end up the same.",
